  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B056",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B023",
    "B025"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B023 to B056. Optical lattice clocks unite a decisive physical idea—the magic-wavelength lattice—with a sustained experimental realization that has been independently reproduced and used as a quantitative measurement capability. Katori and Ye offer a compact concept-to-realization allocation, subject to historical review of other important contributors. For a final choice, this combination of maturity, reproducibility, physical ingenuity, and usable fundamental-measurement reach is more securely delimited than my former preference.",
    "case_against": "The eventual consequences of lattice clocks for time standards and fundamental discoveries are still developing, and the broader optical-clock field includes contributors beyond the proposed pair. The citation must therefore be confined to the invention and development of optical lattice clocks, not to all subsequent precision-clock advances.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce favors B056 because independent long-term reproduction establishes a durable measurement capability and because Katori and Ye provide a comparatively defensible attribution.",
        "response": "I find this assessment persuasive. From the standpoint of quantitative physics, repeated performance across laboratories is strong evidence that a new physical measurement principle has become robust rather than remaining an exceptional demonstration. It does not substitute for fundamental insight, but here it confirms that insight in an unusually compelling way."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll identifies Katori's magic-wavelength lattice concept and Ye's pioneering experimental development as the coherent core of B056, while noting the need to assess wider field credit.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts. The division between a trapping principle that suppresses a central systematic limitation and its decisive experimental development gives the proposed pair a clear rationale. Before an award, the historical boundary must be tested carefully; that necessary review is nevertheless more tractable than resolving the distinct histories combined in B023."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that B023 is exceptionally mature but that its common phase language risks joining historically distinct discoveries too loosely.",
        "response": "This confirms my remaining reservation about my initial choice. The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase share deep physical consequences and remain a leading alternative, but I cannot yet state one citation and two-person allocation that does not risk conflating their separate priority histories. B056 is presently the more exact public configuration."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether documentary historical review can justify limiting the lattice-clock achievement to Katori and Ye while accounting fairly for other major contributors.",
      "Whether B023 can ultimately be defined narrowly enough to recognize observable quantum phase without conflating the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
